            <ArticleTitle>Increased left ventricular non-uniformity in hypertensive patients with angina.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>809-13</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine how hypertension affects the left ventricular diastolic function of patients with effort angina.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We performed radionuclide ventriculography in 14 patients with isolated stenosis of the left anterior descending coronary artery and in seven healthy volunteers.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Neither global nor regional peak filling rate differed significantly between the patients with (n = 6) and without (n = 8) hypertension, although the left ventricular asynchronous index was significantly increased (P &lt; 0.05) in the hypertensive patients (87 +/- 20 ms), compared with the normotensive patients (59 +/- 22 ms). This index was significantly (P &lt; 0.05) correlated with left ventricular mass index in all participants.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Hypertension increased the diastolic non-uniformity of the left ventricle in patients with effort angina.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
